Vannier and Chen J.-Y., 2005 Early Cambrian Food Chain: New Evidence from Fossil Aggregates in the Maotianshan Shale Biota, SW China

:...detailed fossil evidence led Zhu ''et al.'' (2002) to consider the as pelagic, possibly gregarious animals. For example, ''Stellostomites'' had a soft medusiform body with a poorly sclerotized soft disk and radiating lobes that may have functioned as deformable gas chambers analogous to those of modern jellyfishes (Zhu et al., 2002). One of the most conspicuous features of ''Stellostomites'' is a coiled sac that accommodated the alimentary canal of the animal (assumed reniform stomach pouch typically preserved as a black film of organic carbon and iron oxide). The circumoral tentacles of ''Stellostomites'' and ''Pararotadiscus'' suggest that eldoniids were mid-water predators. Eldoniids are not frequent, but form the nucleus of numerous concentric aggregates found exclusively at Shankoucun.

